{"authored" [{"question" "q-80455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-12) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & 5 \\\\ b & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of  $\\frac{a}{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "9fd00ad623a118e795c7170bf964bf1f13f18b4d981a3322f8f1a49c30be89ad" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b" "basis" 2369 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "